## Setup

Given $n = \overline{a_k a_{k-1}\cdots a_1 a_0}$, we have $f(n) = \prod_{j=0}^{k} p_j^{a_j}$. So $f$ maps a number's digits (right-to-left) to exponents of successive primes. The inverse $f^{-1}(m)$ exists iff every exponent in $m$'s prime factorization lies in $\{0,\ldots,9\}$, and then:
$$f^{-1}(m) = \sum_{j\ge 0} v_{p_j}(m)\cdot 10^j$$
where $v_{p_j}(m)$ is the exponent of $p_j$ in $m$'s factorization.

## All $i\in\{1,\ldots,1000\}$ have $f^{-1}(i)$ defined

For $i\le 1000$: the largest prime power is $2^9=512$ (since $2^{10}=1024>1000$). For $p_1=3$: $3^6=729<1000<3^7$. For larger primes, max exponents are even smaller. So every exponent is $\le 9$, hence $f^{-1}(i)$ is well-defined for all $i=1,\ldots,1000$.

## Reducing the sum

$$\sum_{i=1}^{1000} f^{-1}(i) = \sum_{i=1}^{1000}\sum_{j\ge 0} v_{p_j}(i)\cdot 10^j = \sum_{j\ge 0} 10^j \underbrace{\left(\sum_{i=1}^{1000} v_{p_j}(i)\right)}_{S_j}$$

Since $10^j \equiv 0 \pmod{1000}$ for $j\ge 3$, only $S_0, S_1, S_2$ matter:
$$\text{Answer} = (S_0 + 10\,S_1 + 100\,S_2)\bmod 1000$$

## Computing $S_0, S_1, S_2$ via Legendre's formula

$S_j = \sum_{i=1}^{1000} v_{p_j}(i) = \sum_{k\ge 1}\left\lfloor \frac{1000}{p_j^k}\right\rfloor$.

**$S_0 = v_2(1000!)$:**
$$500+250+125+62+31+15+7+3+1 = 994$$

**$S_1 = v_3(1000!)$:**
$$333+111+37+12+4+1 = 498$$

**$S_2 = v_5(1000!)$:**
$$200+40+8+1 = 249$$

## Final computation

$$S_0 + 10\,S_1 + 100\,S_2 = 994 + 4980 + 24900 = 30874$$

$$30874 \bmod 1000 = \boxed{874}$$

**Verification of the formula** with the given example: $f(2015)=7^2\cdot5^0\cdot3^1\cdot2^5=4704$, and $f^{-1}(4704)=5+1\cdot10+0\cdot100+2\cdot1000=2015$. ✓

The remainder when $\sum_{i=1}^{1000} f^{-1}(i)$ is divided by $1000$ is $\mathbf{874}$.

